CORE
x_literal_widening1.sv
--bound 2
^\[main\.p0\] always main\.in == 0 \|\| main\.r == main\.r: PROVED up to bound 2$
^EXIT=0$
^SIGNAL=0$
--
^warning: ignoring
^Invariant check failed
--
Assigning a sized x literal (1'bx) to a wider variable used to abort ebmc
with an invariant violation in the flattening back-end:

  File: flattening/boolbv_typecast.cpp:432 function: type_conversion
  Condition: src_width >= dest_width
  Reason: cannot extend bv-typed bitvector

The x literal has type verilog_unsignedbv[1], and the assignment emits a
widening typecast to unsignedbv[32]. During Verilog lowering, the
four-valued-to-two-valued conversion extracted the aval as a plain bv,
so widening it to the destination width was left to the solver, which
refuses to extend a bv-typed bitvector. The lowering now reinterprets
the aval as the source's underlying (un)signed type, so the widening is
a proper zero/sign extension (1800-2017 11.8.3). Found in picorv32.v
(decoded_imm <= 1'bx in the default branch of the immediate decoder).
